1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,000
When a tenancy ends, the landlord keeps part of the deposit,
and nobody remembers what the flat looked like on day one.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:19,000
RentalEase is a simulated Alexa+ voice experience
for tenants and landlords. It answers from the move-in and
move-out records, through its own MCP server.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,000
"Alexa, ask RentalEase whether the scuff was
already there when I moved in."

4
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
My browser heard me, not an Alexa device. The rule assistant
quotes both reports word for word, and each source chip
links to the record.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
It never says who is right.

6
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
"Dispute the wall charge for me."

7
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:22,000
It only prepares a draft. I read the preview, then press
Confirm and save myself. Nothing is recorded until I do.

8
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Switching roles simulates the landlord. He withdraws the
wall charge, and the recorded refund rises by one hundred ringgit.

9
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Agreed is not paid: no money moves here.

10
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:09,000
If I would rather write to my landlord, it drafts a letter
from the same records. It quotes both reports, asks for
evidence, and never says who is liable.

11
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Nothing is sent from here.

12
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Other MCP clients can use the same server. With this case's token,
MCP Inspector lists the six read-only tools.
There is no confirm or payment tool.

13
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:49,000
This builds on my RentalEase final-year project. Voice, the MCP
server and multi-item resolution are new for the hackathon.
Optional Amazon Bedrock support is built in, but this
recording uses the rule assistant.
